Update on red tide: Concentrations in Pinellas, Manatee and Sarasota are removed
Renowned report from the Florida Fish and Wildlife Commission shows that there is a general decrease in concentrations of red tide in parts of the Tampa Bay area.
According to FWCs, scorky flowering conditions are recorded in the areas between Pinella, Manatee, Sarasota, Charlotte and necklines, which indicates declining concentration.
A red algal blossom extends approximately 130 miles coastline from no north Pinellas to the south of the district.
Spikes can be found approximately 10 miles away from the coast of the Hillsborough district, however at fewer locations compared to previous week.
A red tide was also observed in Pasco district.
Over the last week medium concentrations were registered in or off the coast of Pinellas district, low to high concentrations located off the coast of Manatee district, background to high concentrations in or off the coast of the Charlotte district, background to high concentrations in or off the coast of the district Lee and low concentrations in Collier district.
Respiratory difficulties continue to be reported from Pinellas, Manatee, Sarasota, and Collier districts.
There were no number of new respiratory odour problems in north-west Florida last year.
